---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config';
import { tools } from '../data/tools';
import { guides } from '../data/guides';

const organizationSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'AboutPage',
  name: `About ${SITE.name}`,
  url: `${SITE.domain}/about/`,
  mainEntity: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: SITE.publisher,
    url: SITE.domain,
    email: SITE.contactEmail,
  },
};
---

<Base
  title="About DevSnap — Free Browser-Based Developer Tools"
  description="What DevSnap is, who builds it, how the tools run in your browser, and why nothing you paste is ever transmitted to a server."
  canonicalPath="/about/"
  schemas={organizationSchema}
>
  <div class="wrap wrap-narrow">
    <ol class="crumbs">
      <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
      <li>About</li>
    </ol>

    <header class="tool-hero">
      <h1>About {SITE.name}</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A small, independent toolbox for the things developers do twenty times a day
        and nobody wants to install a package for.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose mt-3">
      <h2>What this is</h2>
      <p>
        {SITE.name} is a set of {tools.length} single-purpose tools for formatting,
        converting, inspecting and sharing developer data: JSON, YAML, SQL, XML, CSV,
        regular expressions, JWTs, cron schedules, hashes, diffs, timestamps, QR codes
        and a few odds and ends that turned out to be needed constantly.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is no account, no install step, no trial tier and no dashboard. You open
        a page, you paste something, you get a result, you copy it. That is the whole
        product.
      </p>

      <h2>How the tools work</h2>
      <p>
        Every tool runs as JavaScript in your browser, using the same platform
        primitives your own application would use: the native <code>JSON</code> engine
        for parsing, the Web Crypto API for hashing, the Canvas API for rendering
        images, and the standard URL and base64 utilities for encoding.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing is uploaded. This is not a promise about our intentions, it is an
        architectural property: there is no endpoint that receives your input. If you
        open the browser's network panel while you use a tool, you will see the page
        load and nothing else.
      </p>
      <p>
        The practical consequence is that a payload containing a credential, a customer
        record or an internal hostname stays on your machine, and the tool still works
        with the network switched off.
      </p>

      <h2>What we publish</h2>
      <p>
        Alongside the tools there are {guides.length} longer guides. They exist because
        a tool that shows you a ratio or a field breakdown is more useful when you
        understand why the rule is what it is. Each one covers a single concept —
        reading a JWT, cron semantics, timezone handling, WCAG contrast, UTM naming —
        and is written to be useful even if you never touch the site again.
      </p>

      <h2>How it is funded</h2>
      <p>
        Through advertising. The layout is deliberate about that: the tools are the
        product, and the advertising is kept out of the way of the work. We do not sell
        data, we do not run tracking scripts beyond what the ad network requires, and
        we do not ask for an email address.
      </p>
      <p>
        If a tool is useful enough that you would pay for it, that is worth telling us
        about. The most valuable thing you can send us is a description of what you
        were trying to do and what the tool could not do.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Tools get things wrong. A regex engine that mishandles a flag, a date
        calculation that forgets a daylight saving transition, a formatter that changes
        a string literal — all of these are the kind of thing that silently breaks
        somebody's build.
      </p>
      <p>
        If a tool gives you a wrong answer, <a href="/contact/">tell us</a> with the
        input that produced it. We will fix it and, where the tool has a documented
        limitation, we will say so on the page.
      </p>

      <h2>Relationship to other projects</h2>
      <p>
        {SITE.name} is an independent project. It is not affiliated with, endorsed by
        or sponsored by Google, Adobe, GitHub, Mozilla or any other organisation whose
        name appears in its documentation. Product names and trademarks belong to their
        respective owners and are used here only to describe compatibility.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
